  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 243 AM MST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nEarly morning satellite imagery shows widespread cloud cover \nacross eastern Idaho as an upper-level trough swings through the\narea. Regional radar depicts light-to-moderate precipitation \nacross parts of the Magic Valley and into the Central Mountains,\nwhile areas farther east have observed only occasional light \nsprinkles. High-resolution convective-allowing models (CAMs) \nindicate this precipitation shield shifting eastward toward \ndaybreak, with light precipitation overspreading the remainder \nof the forecast area over the course of the day. Overall \nprecipitation amounts will remain generally light due to limited\natmospheric moisture. Expect 0.20 to 0.40 inches of liquid \nequivalent across the Wood River Valley and adjacent mountains, \nand up to 0.30 inches from around Pomerelle eastward through the\nBear River Range, though some model spread remains regarding \nthe exact areal coverage of those higher totals. Locations \noutside of these favored terrain zones are expected to receive \naround 0.10 inches or less. Snowfall will remain generally above\n7,000 feet, keeping impactful accumulations confined above pass\nlevel at 1 to 3 inches, with localized upslope totals up to 5 \ninches across parts of the Sawtooths, Boulders, and Pioneers. \nCold advection and persistent cloud cover will make daytime \nhighs markedly colder today, generally staying in the 40s \nareawide.\n\n\nEastern Idaho will reside between departing and incoming upper-\nlevel troughs on Sunday. Dry conditions will return under partly\ncloudy skies, allowing afternoon temperatures to rebound by\naround 5 degrees compared to Saturday's readings. The next\nPacific storm system shifts slowly inland on Monday. The first\nhalf of the day looks dry for most locations, with precipitation\ngradually overspreading the region as the afternoon progresses\nunder temperatures similar to Sunday. This system will drive a\nnotably colder air mass into eastern Idaho behind it, setting up\nTuesday as a day where snowfall could reach much of the region,\nincluding down to valley floors. Exact snow levels remain\ndifficult to pinpoint at this range; higher elevations will\ncertainly see light accumulations, and light accumulations\ncannot be completely ruled out in the valleys. At this stage,\nhowever, lower elevations appear hard-pressed to see substantial\naccumulation, though trends will be monitored closely in\nupcoming forecast cycles. The mid-to-late portion of next week\nlooks to remain unsettled with continued broad troughing over\nthe Pacific Northwest and periodic waves of embedded moisture\npushing through the Intermountain West. Daytime valley highs\nwill likely remain restricted to the upper 30s and lower 40s\nfrom Tuesday straight into next weekend, accompanied by\ncontinued daily chances for light snow.",
